  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "intervals-introduction",
      "title": "Intervals — Introduction",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** theory | **Tier:** intro | **Difficulty:** 2/10\n**Prerequisites:** Major Scales — Introduction\n**Tags:** intervals, ear-training"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "An **interval** is the distance between two pitches. Naming intervals precisely is the bridge between scales and harmony."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "size-and-quality",
      "title": "Size and quality",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Count letter names for size (C to E = third). Measure semitones for quality: major, minor, perfect, augmented, diminished."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-intervals-from-c",
      "title": "Core intervals from C",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "| Interval | Semitones | Example |\n|----------|-----------|---------|\n| m2 | 1 | C–D♭ |\n| M2 | 2 | C–D |\n| m3 | 3 | C–E♭ |\n| M3 | 4 | C–E |\n| P4 | 5 | C–F |\n| A4/d5 | 6 | C–F♯ / C–G♭ |\n| P5 | 7 | C–G |\n| M6 | 9 | C–A |\n| M7 | 11 | C–B |\n| P8 | 12 | C–C |"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "entities": [],
  "entity_order": [],
  "playlist": [],
  "tunes": [],
  "key_points": [],
  "reading_list": [],
  "quiz_markdown": "### Q1. C to E is what size interval?\n\n- A) Third\n- B) Second\n- C) Fourth\n- D) Fifth\n\n**Answer:** A\n\n### Q2. A perfect fifth spans seven semitones from C.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q3. Minor third has four semitones from C to E♭.\n\n**Answer:** False — three semitones.\n\n### Q4. Octave equals twelve semitones.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q5. Interval quality includes major and minor.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q6. C to F is a perfect fourth.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q7. C to B is a major seventh.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q8. Intervals are measured between two pitches.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q9. Letter count and semitone count both matter.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q10. M3 is wider than m3 by one semitone.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q11. P8 is also called octave.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q12. Ear training builds on interval recognition.\n\n**Answer:** True",
